{{/*

DankMaterialShell settings — SEED ONLY.

`create_` prefix: chezmoi writes this file once, if it is absent, and then never
touches it again. That is deliberate and required — DMS *owns* settings.json at
runtime (SettingsData.saveSettings() rewrites the whole file whenever any
setting changes, and it can even flip the file read-only). Shipping it as a
plain chezmoi file would turn every DMS settings change into permanent drift.
Same precedent as dot_config/niri/create_colors.kdl.

`private_` prefix (i.e. mode 0600): NOT a secrecy claim, a drift fix. `create_`
stops chezmoi rewriting the CONTENT but it still asserts the MODE on every
apply, and DMS writes this file back as 0600. Without the prefix, `chezmoi
status` reported a permanent `MM` on settings.json that no amount of re-adding
could clear, which is exactly the kind of standing noise that hides a real diff.
0600 is what the file actually is, so declare that.

Because DMS rewrites it, this is a partial file on purpose: SettingsStore.parse()
applies only the keys present here and leaves every other property at its QML
default. `configVersion` is deliberately OMITTED — DMS 1.5.3 is at config
version 13 and master is already at 16, so pinning a number here would be wrong
for some build; leaving it out makes DMS run its migration chain over this file
(verified: every migration guards on keys this seed does not set, so all of them
are no-ops) and then rewrite the file stamped with whatever version it is.

DESIGN: in this session DMS owns the shell, the lock screen AND idle. hyprlock
and hypridle are not mixed in — hypridle is runtime-masked by
~/.local/bin/hyprland-dms, and nothing starts hyprlock (hyprlock.service has no
[Install] section, so it only ever ran on demand from hypridle's lock_cmd).
`loginctlLockIntegration` is therefore left at its default of TRUE, which is
what makes Super+Escape (`loginctl lock-session`, unchanged in hyprland.conf)
reach DMS's lock screen. There is no second ext-session-lock client to race.

1. LOCK AUTHENTICATION — the FIDO2 stick, armed immediately.

   DMS's native security-key mode (enableU2f + u2fMode) treats the key as a
   separate factor that you start on demand with the passkey button, which costs
   a keypress on every unlock. Instead DMS is put in "custom primary PAM" mode
   pointing at /etc/pam.d/dms-fido2, whose stack is `pam_u2f sufficient` then
   `pam_unix required`. Because lockPamInlineU2f says that stack already
   contains pam_u2f, DMS suppresses its own factor UI (Pam.qml:
   `u2fSuppressedByPrimaryPam`) and the key is armed the instant the screen
   locks — a bare touch unlocks, password still works as fallback. Identical to
   hyprlock's behaviour today, reusing the same proven stack.

   ⚠️ This is silent when it breaks. DMS only honours lockPamPath if it can READ
   the file (`customPamActive = lockPamPath !== "" && customPamWatcher.loaded`);
   otherwise it falls back down its chain to the service named `login` — i.e.
   exactly into the pam_sss double-prompt trap that /etc/pam.d/dms-fido2 exists
   to avoid, with nothing shown on screen. Install the file with
   `mise run bootstrap:pam-dms`, which asserts both readability and that this
   path matches.

   enableU2f/enableFprint are left off on purpose: the primary stack does the
   key, and there is no fprintd reader enrolled for the lock screen to use.

2. IDLE — DMS's IdleService, mirroring the hypridle timings it replaces.

   Timeouts are in SECONDS (Quickshell IdleMonitor; the 86400 fallback in
   IdleService.qml confirms the unit). Mapped from hypridle.conf:

     hypridle 600s  -> lock                     acLockTimeout            600
     hypridle 630s  -> dpms off, 30s after lock  acPostLockMonitorTimeout  30
     hypridle before_sleep_cmd -> lock          lockBeforeSuspend        true

   acMonitorTimeout stays 0 (disabled): hypridle has no "monitors off *before*
   lock" step, and IdleService disables the pre-lock monitor anyway once
   postLockMonitorActive is set. Battery values equal the AC ones because
   hypridle.conf does not distinguish AC from battery.

   ⚠️ acSuspendTimeout MUST STAY 0. hypridle never suspended on idle, and that
   is deliberate: on 2026-08-12 an unattended timed hibernate wedged inside a
   closed backpack and cooked the machine for 44 minutes. Suspending is a
   deliberate act here, never a timer. See the Super+CTRL+Escape comment in
   hyprland.conf.

   NOT carried over: hypridle's 300s `brightnessctl -s set 10%` dim step. DMS's
   IdleService has no brightness-dim stage, so that is genuinely lost. Its
   fadeToLock (a 5s fade before locking, cancelled by any input) is left at its
   default of enabled and is the nearest thing to a pre-lock warning.

3. THEMING — stays toggle-color-scheme's.

   DMS ships wallpaper-derived Material 3 theming, and *every* template flag
   defaults ON: matugenTemplateGtk, matugenTemplateHyprland, ...Ghostty,
   ...Qt6ct, ...Emacs, ...Vscode. That is exactly the fan-out
   toggle-color-scheme owns as the single source of truth, and several of those
   templates write files chezmoi manages — so leaving this on would cause drift
   in the DEFAULT session too, not only in the DMS one. These two master
   switches are guarded in the QML (`!runDmsMatugenTemplates` -> return), and
   matugen is additionally excluded at the dnf level (.chezmoidata/packages.yaml
   -> packages.dnfExclude), so the templates are stopped twice: no switch and
   no binary.

See ~/Documents/docs/hyprland-dms-quickshell-session.md.

*/ -}}
{
  "lockPamPath": "/etc/pam.d/dms-fido2",
  "lockPamInlineU2f": true,
  "lockPamInlineFprint": false,
  "lockPamExternallyManaged": false,
  "enableU2f": false,
  "enableFprint": false,

  "acLockTimeout": 600,
  "acPostLockMonitorTimeout": 30,
  "acMonitorTimeout": 0,
  "acSuspendTimeout": 0,
  "batteryLockTimeout": 600,
  "batteryPostLockMonitorTimeout": 30,
  "batteryMonitorTimeout": 0,
  "batterySuspendTimeout": 0,
  "lockBeforeSuspend": true,

  "runDmsMatugenTemplates": false,
  "runUserMatugenTemplates": false
}
